                      <p class=3D"corner-intro">A collection of web design =
and development news and thoughts from CodePen's own Chris Coyier.</p>=0D=
=0A                          </mj-raw>=0D=0A                          <h3>L=
ovingly Esoteric CSS</h3>=0A<p>Can you reason out the difference between a =
selector doing <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/SorB0ntHEwUH-N3=
TxbiCjA~~/AABEfhA~/55OpLeuRTFxQSWXsHpFoDZAmhk3DIVJprkK7w2bl8CkdQGtAJ0-zGXN8=
pnnR8C5OKAKEynh7YP3CYyTx-vzfQsxkIJgnIDtWOmef3MUE7QAv3m3Z_jQe6-0nZw9iHcNwB_O=
Og5vkha75sseL337W9j1CJNdvFcq3OoosfMc3EKkOw2mxUEbOs_HOVKSIU7Zz2QdmEP1OKLyEuj=
QOUc-bSldRK0klfBmoiMvz4atuCZ8baLRrL66GfzXJcnH00BtU"><code>:has(:not) vs :no=
t(:has)</code></a>=3F Kilian Valkhof wrote up that post I just linked to. I=
 like how he teaches the way to think it out is to write out all the <em>im=
plied</em> selectors at work. For instance:</p>=0A<pre><code class=3D"css">=
.card:has(:not(img)) {=0A}=0A.card:not(:has(img)) {=0A}=0A</code></pre>=0A=
<p>is really:</p>=0A<pre><code class=3D"css">.card:has(*:not(img)) {=0A}=0A=
.card:not(.card:has(img)) {=0A}=0A</code></pre>=0A<p>The latter becomes a =
lot more clear to me:</p>=0A<ol>=0A<li>Select all the stuff in a <code>.car=
d</code> that isn=E2=80=99t an <code>img</code>.</li>=0A<li>Select the card=
 as long as it doesn=E2=80=99t contain an <code>img</code>.</li>=0A</ol>=0A=
<p>Super different. I love stuff like this. Like Sudoku puzzles for CSS ner=
ds.</p>=0A<hr />=0A<p>Speaking of <code>:has()</code>, <a href=3D"https://g=
o.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/gFylR3EQSouL6xFLs163GA~~/AABEfhA~/srYqyHfjx93biZzw=
R63iQIM9yd57Jm7QRHd9_Bbu4iPH82GlJamegPEGfS8rzMsswTikuTSYfbMUwsf4LzABG8NGrQB=
yxpTtW8Y0WQ3_yvEZztQQcxOP5JxKmYOkyj6v9DVAptRlzTR89VrUoLKj8Q_17uZKHxxKukwSJr=
7n-CZ180aSSh0wlGjgbha6T3P5gxIv5swHxEqvr0lcy5AhGg~~">Heydon gets a bit philo=
sophical with a =E2=80=9CSidebar=E2=80=9D layout</a>, explaining how it can=
 get a lot more flexible when we think of how to handle it with any particu=
lar layout that <em>just so happens to have a sidebar</em>. And <em>just so=
 happens</em> is a specialty of <code>:has()</code>, polished up with a nic=
e dollop of <code>@container</code>.</p>=0A<hr />=0A<p>I didn=E2=80=99t kno=
w this was coming, but I love it, and it makes intuitive sense to me. Una e=
xplains in <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/ejaSSoMzr3jImAe-xWH=
GDw~~/AABEfhA~/sXXXsIixRLkT0JAtphLgTyTK3hCm1CxkwnzyTomR47lG23wNlw2EJaLvts5K=
U3EtJwRk6qb0HYzAEMTcGocel7CiS-q_APm3s7gNBHPtEVxtk0H3h3dVWQtgWu-PSynJ3olfGct=
m3OX3KIGu-wSJJg~~">Range Syntax for Style Queries</a>.</p>=0A<pre><code cla=
ss=3D"css">/* Range query for rain percent (new) */=0A@container style(--ra=
in-percent &gt; 45%) {=0A  .weather-card {=0A  background: linear-gradient(=
140deg, skyblue, lightblue);=0A  }=0A}=0A</code></pre>=0A<p>My brain goes r=
ight now how <code>line-height</code> often needs to get sucked in when the=
 <code>font-size</code> goes up. So assuming those are custom properties po=
wered, something like this could be cool:</p>=0A<pre><code class=3D"css">=
=0A@container style(--font-size &gt; 2rem) {=0A=09line-height: 1;=0A}=0A</c=
ode></pre>=0A<p>But my enthusiasm is tempered by the fact that you can=E2=
=80=99t do a container query on the same element you set the custom propert=
y on. So like this doesn=E2=80=99t work:</p>=0A<pre><code class=3D"css">=0A=
h1,=0Ah2,=0Ah3 {=0A=09font-size: var(--header-font-size, 1.5rem);=0A=09@con=
tainer style(--header-font-size &gt; 2rem) {=0A=09=09line-height: 1;=0A=09}=
=0A}=0A=0Ah1 {=0A=09--header-font-size: 3rem;=0A}=0A</code></pre>=0A<p>You =
can see what I mean <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/MBATrXbdol=
GTw2aJptb4Cg~~/AABEfhA~/ndaliovp6FwC9su9KvidmuZmDwWJ-yAG5J7dHqxFwswDg4HlbJw=
NAPrD25rBgj8KrWfUrP0HRlZYgSEqFtnouy3Yw-igoVLmQRqA8eEqMl3bxaci1wqUi_Nj0ngsyY=
sIEBp64JVXbXZ2hskGiP5XOAo8YbWRjJ0pHBa-ei1_MjTjDvUe04f1T73TtaRlS_IN">here wi=
th a working version</a>.</p>=0A<p>Because I=E2=80=99m setting <code>--head=
er-font-size</code> at the same =E2=80=9Clevel=E2=80=9D as I=E2=80=99m test=
ing it with<code> @container</code> it just doesn=E2=80=99t work. It would =
have to be set at least one DOM level higher, which is probably not what I=
=E2=80=99d be doing on an element like this. Oh well, it=E2=80=99s still co=
ol.</p>=0A<hr />=0A<p>I appreciate <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com=
/f/a/8KNQpC9r7CUSuKg2AwIzWw~~/AABEfhA~/KbO-iO49WRJgJUvK47mAfkhbgFn4LbcCzM-M=
7FfFt_bNIyeRBlv2A8GME127uhgUFVE2P1TgOGrW_S4amcUMj1lnOS4rcxjLmyQG-e2RieTnb1e=
XLazJc5onG5UJ7F-A3sBGo7dbJ1eBMkPiqZELig~~">Roma Komarov=E2=80=99s obsessing=
 about a tiny detail here</a>. This website <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpost=
mail2.com/f/a/oVdJHjlIs3R7tO8y0TO9SA~~/AABEfhA~/TSKlRVU_4-l6vtkj_G8OOPIuRnT=
x84zN0ozvCPs4O_Vhd4txQ5ObBbQpFU7lFS3YwuDr769YR7cZgKxpagabR2WT6c7KMT02v7qV_9=
HyXKXHj_M5Cx1XkTEOYQyNTggX">Ink &amp; Switch</a> has these kinda rough hand=
-drawn underlines on it. But when they <em>wrap</em> the wrapping edge is =
=E2=80=9Chard cut=E2=80=9D. But it needn=E2=80=99t be so. The CSS property =
<code>box-decoration-break: clone</code> fixes it entirely. Beautiful. You =
can kinda forgive someone not knowing this as the browser support isn=E2=80=
=99t perfect. And more, the fact that it starts with <code>box-</code> kind=
a makes you think it=E2=80=99s gonna be related to <code>box-shadow</code> =
but really it=E2=80=99s (get this): <code>border-radius</code>, <code>borde=
r-image</code>, <code>box-shadow</code>, and <code>background</code>. That=
=E2=80=99s some good bar trivia right there for you and all your CSS buds.<=
/p>=0A<hr />=0A<p>My toxic trait is that I like to declare winners of thing=
s. Like how <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/y2kimQl2CLSeiMouEK=
y3kg~~/AABEfhA~/3t8LnwTu9S4GbuPVgszvs_VSwjoM2XM76Hk6Hf62S9shYInYJw4aewIvGhl=
ZXJ8rR_PabcrCi6flhK-f6yVwuMpDH0N23KC381LO_zC2AXRWeWcRf3ywJKM_0mPd2Y3IpKK94-=
ibUB7_8nw-0jeQL5JLbPW14vjhyW3_1IAgEuQ~">Apple won with grid-lanes</a> =F0=
=9F=98=AC. Or how <code>oklch()</code> is the winning modern color format. =
Instead of giving those topics the nuance they deserve. I also have a lot o=
f muscle memory for just using <code>rem</code> units everywhere, consideri=
ng them the winning unit. But that one is almost certainly too narrow of an=
 opinion. In fact, it=E2=80=99s probably not any one particular unit, but a=
s Miriam says <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/6RKSALzvVKxZNzhD=
UFo5Eg~~/AABEfhA~/vD-n63E7Sk8B3AELWC4aZa_KyZc-pm0Q7a3RCg8wHzVbgq1QiQs6aFsrE=
8NdBnjXPeaATHdNvZPbP4L77firmW7lHy-ZGJ01f0hodWk6aiTc7ueV9qBdTfaY7dc4HXNbYd8o=
l7u7ZxhLk1DZxMVh9A~~">The Best CSS Unit Might Be a Combination: We don=E2=
=80=99t have to choose between px and rem for spacing</a>.</p>=0A<pre><code=
 class=3D"css">.card {=0A=09/* use 1lh, unless we run out of space */=0A=09=
--min: min(1lh, 2vi);=0A=0A=09/* round-up to the nearest half-line */=0A=09=
--nearest-half: round(up, 2vi, 0.5lh);=0A}=0A</code></pre>=0D=0A           =
